---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '~/components/PageHeader.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '~/config/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Accessibility"
  description="Mindful aims for WCAG 2.2 level AA. What we have built in, what we test, the gaps we know about, and how to report a problem."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-[52rem]">
    <PageHeader
      title="A site more people can use, comfortably."
      crumbs={[{ label: 'Accessibility' }]}
    />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        We want Mindful to work for people who read with a keyboard, a screen reader, zoomed-in text
        or a small phone. Our target is the
        <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/">Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2</a>,
        level AA.
      </p>

      <h2>What is in place</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Every page has one main heading, a header, a main region and a footer, and a &ldquo;Skip
          to content&rdquo; link as the first thing you can tab to.
        </li>
        <li>
          The menu is a native dialog. It opens and closes from the keyboard, closes with Escape,
          and puts focus back on the menu button when it closes.
        </li>
        <li>
          Focus is always visible, and the sticky header leaves room so it never covers the element
          you have tabbed to.
        </li>
        <li>The link to the page you are on is marked as the current page.</li>
        <li>
          Colours follow your system&rsquo;s light or dark setting, and animations are switched off
          when you ask your system to reduce motion.
        </li>
        <li>Essay photos have alternative text.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How we check</h2>
      <p>
        Code is linted with the jsx-a11y accessibility rules. Automated browser tests, run on a
        desktop and a Pixel 5 screen size, check the page landmarks, the skip link, the menu dialog
        with Escape, and the footer links.
      </p>

      <h2>Known gaps</h2>
      <p>
        The essay photos are placeholders from Picsum, not pictures chosen for each essay. Many
        essays have no written image description, so their photo&rsquo;s alternative text is the
        essay title, which tells you little about the photo itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Automated tests only catch some problems. Your combination of device, browser and assistive
        technology may turn up barriers we have not seen.
      </p>

      <h2>Report a problem</h2>
      <p>
        If something on Mindful is hard to use, please open an issue on
        <a href={siteConfig.issuesUrl}>the Mindful repository on GitHub</a>. Tell us the page, what
        you were trying to do, what got in the way, and the device and assistive technology you use
        if you are happy to share it.
      </p>

      <p class="meta">This statement was last reviewed on 1 November 2026.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
